## Finish

On completion, record the outcome:

    nat complete-slice 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --branch <branch> --summary '<what you did>' \
        --pr-description '<title line>

<what the PR does and why>'

That records the branch you pushed and hands the slice back for review,
writing the summary onto its page: what you did, key decisions, follow-ups
worth queueing. It leaves the slice in progress on purpose — approving it
on the board is what opens the pull request and marks it Done.

`--pr-description` is what that pull request is opened with: its first
line is the title and the rest the body, so write it ready to publish —
what the change does and why, for whoever reviews it on GitHub, not a
report of your session. Pass `--pr-description -` to read it from stdin
when it is too long for an argument, and give `--summary` as a flag then.

Leave `--branch` off when the slice produced no branch — a docs or
research slice — and it is marked Done there and then, with no pull
request to describe. A summary too long for one argument can be piped in
on stdin instead of passing `--summary`.

If you cannot complete it, say what stopped you and leave the slice
in progress, so nobody else picks it up on top of your work:

    nat complete-slice 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --blocked --summary '<what is blocking>'

